nt_line << (word_len*8)) | word_buf\n                //      // current_line_len = current_line_len + word_len\n                //   end\n                // \n                // We must also handle the case where word_len > 20 (truncated during accumulation).\n                // \n                // To make this synthesizable in Verilog without dynamic shifts:\n                // We can use a case statement or if-else for word_len.\n                // \n                // Let's implement the word processing logic in a separate sequential block,\n                // triggered by a 'word_done' pulse.\n            end\n        end\n    end\n\n    // Re-implementing WRAP_LINES logic robustly:\n    // We will modify the FSM block to handle word boundaries cleanly.\n    // We need to detect the transition from word char to space (or line end).\n    // \n    // Actually, the simplest robust way for Verilog synthesis is:\n    // 1. Accumulate chars in a buffer.\n    // 2. When space is found:\n    //    - If buffer + current_line > W -> Save current_line, Start New Line with buffer.\n    //    - Else -> Append buffer to current_line.\n    // 3. Reset buffer.\n    // \n    // The code in the FSM block for WRAP_LINES will be updated to perform these actions.\n    // We need to handle the \"Save current_line\" and \"Append\" operations.\n    // These operations involve bit concatenation/shifting.\n    // \n    // Given the instruction to return only one module, we must put everything in one file.\n    // The logic for `append` and `start new line` is best handled by a combinational output for the line buffer.\n    // But for simplicity and adherence to the \"one always block\" style often used in tutorials,\n    // we can do it in the clocked block by spending cycles.\n    // \n    // Given the \"Max 20,000 cycles\" constraint, we can afford to take 1 cycle per decision.\n    // \n    // Revised WRAP_LINES State Logic (inside the main FSM):\n    // \n    // if (parse_char_idx < 80) ...\n    // else (End of line) -> trigger word processing if word active.\n    // \n    // We need a flag `process_word_now`.\n    // \n    // Let's use the `word_ready` signal from the combinational block.\n    // \n    // We will create a combinational block that sets `word_ready` when we see a space or EOL.\n    // But wait, the FSM block reads/writes `parsing_word`.\n    // If we drive `parsing_word` from `word_ready`, it's combinational loop.\n    // \n    // Correct Approach:\n    // In the FSM block, when we see a space:\n    // 1. Set `pending_word_buf` and `pending_word_len`.\n    // 2. Set `do_word_process` flag.\n    // 3. In the same cycle (or next), calculate the new line state.\n    // \n    // Let's assume the FSM updates `pending_word` signals, and a combinational logic calculates the update.\n    // \n    // Actually, let's stick to the pseudo-code logic and make it purely sequential with sub-states.\n    // \n    // We will add a state `WRAP_PROCESS_WORD` between characters.\n    // But we are processing chars in a loop. \n    // \n    // Let's go back to the single always block and add the logic there.\n    // \n    // To handle the wrapping logic correctly in the FSM block:\n    // \n    // We need to process the word when it ends.\n    // When we are in `WRAP_LINES` and we detect a space (or EOL):\n    //   - We have `word_buf` and `word_len`.\n    //   - We need to decide if `current_line_len + word_len <= W`.\n    //   - If yes: `current_line = (current_line << (word_len*8)) | word_buf` (conceptually, actually it's append).\n    //     Wait, if we store chars in MSB to LSB or LSB to MSB?\n    //     Let's store MSB first (char 0 at top bits).\n    //     So `current_line` is [19:0]. Char 0 is [19:12], Char 1 is [11:4] (if 2 chars).\n    //     Actually, `char_i` is at `[19-i*8 +: 8]` if we fill top-down.\n    //     Or `char_i` is at `[i*8 +: 8]` if we fill bottom-up.\n    //     Let's use `[i*8 +: 8]` (left padding). \n    //     Example